1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,005
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,455 --> 00:00:07,600
This is High School Physics, Unit 1,
Measurement and Motion Basics, Part 2. In

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,821
this video, we'll cover four topics, each
with a worked example.

4
00:00:12,271 --> 00:00:13,359
Acceleration.

5
00:00:13,809 --> 00:00:16,859
Motion Graphs: Position Time and Velocity
Time.

6
00:00:17,309 --> 00:00:18,760
The Kinematic Equations.

7
00:00:19,210 --> 00:00:20,042
Free Fall.

8
00:00:20,492 --> 00:00:24,247
And at the end, one bigger problem that
puts the whole unit together.

9
00:00:24,697 --> 00:00:25,785
Let's get started.

10
00:00:27,433 --> 00:00:29,886
Press the gas, and you're pushed back into
your seat.

11
00:00:30,336 --> 00:00:32,128
Hit the brakes, and you lurch forward.

12
00:00:32,578 --> 00:00:36,653
In both cases, your velocity is changing.
That's acceleration.

13
00:00:37,803 --> 00:00:42,944
Acceleration is the change in velocity,
divided by the time the change takes.

14
00:00:43,394 --> 00:00:48,386
Its unit is meters per second, per second,
written as meters per second squared.

15
00:00:49,536 --> 00:00:53,675
A car goes from zero to twenty meters per
second in five seconds.

16
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:58,964
Its acceleration is twenty divided by
five, which is four meters per second

17
00:00:58,964 --> 00:00:59,480
squared.

18
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:03,706
That means every second, it gains four
meters per second of speed.

19
00:01:04,856 --> 00:01:07,842
Acceleration is a vector, so its direction
matters.

20
00:01:08,292 --> 00:01:13,178
If the acceleration points the same way as
the velocity, the object speeds up.

21
00:01:13,628 --> 00:01:16,678
If it points the opposite way, the object
slows down.

22
00:01:17,828 --> 00:01:22,202
A bike moving at ten meters per second
brakes to a stop in four seconds.

23
00:01:22,652 --> 00:01:28,070
The change in velocity is zero minus ten,
which is negative ten meters per second.

24
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:33,174
Divide by four seconds, and the
acceleration is negative two point five

25
00:01:33,174 --> 00:01:34,878
meters per second squared.

26
00:01:35,328 --> 00:01:38,570
The minus sign means it points backward,
against the motion.

27
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:41,086
Now try one yourself.

28
00:01:41,536 --> 00:01:46,363
A train speeds up from six meters per
second to thirty meters per second in

29
00:01:46,363 --> 00:01:48,938
eight seconds. What is its acceleration?

30
00:01:49,388 --> 00:01:50,583
Pause the video now.

31
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:55,953
The change in velocity is thirty minus
six, which is twenty four meters per

32
00:01:55,953 --> 00:02:00,676
second. Twenty four divided by eight is
three meters per second squared.

33
00:02:00,976 --> 00:02:04,133
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

34
00:02:05,283 --> 00:02:10,423
A car moving at twenty five meters per
second brakes steadily, and stops in five

35
00:02:10,423 --> 00:02:10,937
seconds.

36
00:02:11,387 --> 00:02:15,589
Find its acceleration, and its speed two
seconds after braking starts.

37
00:02:16,739 --> 00:02:20,793
Acceleration is the change in velocity
divided by the time.

38
00:02:21,243 --> 00:02:25,509
Then the velocity after any time is the
start velocity plus a t.

39
00:02:26,659 --> 00:02:31,886
The givens: start velocity twenty five,
final velocity zero, time five seconds.

40
00:02:32,336 --> 00:02:38,002
Acceleration: zero minus twenty five,
divided by five, is minus five meters per

41
00:02:38,002 --> 00:02:39,077
second squared.

42
00:02:39,527 --> 00:02:44,617
After two seconds: twenty five plus minus
five times two, which is fifteen meters

43
00:02:44,617 --> 00:02:45,309
per second.

44
00:02:46,459 --> 00:02:52,072
The acceleration is minus five meters per
second squared. After two seconds, the car

45
00:02:52,072 --> 00:02:54,544
is down to fifteen meters per second.

46
00:02:54,994 --> 00:02:59,902
The minus sign means the acceleration
points opposite to the motion. Every

47
00:02:59,902 --> 00:03:02,887
second, the car loses five meters per
second.

48
00:03:03,337 --> 00:03:08,308
Tip: always subtract final minus initial.
Doing it backward flips the sign.

49
00:03:08,758 --> 00:03:13,809
Tip: negative acceleration doesn't always
mean slowing down. It means pointing in

50
00:03:13,809 --> 00:03:15,243
the negative direction.

51
00:03:16,393 --> 00:03:21,594
Your turn. A bike speeds up from three to
nine meters per second in four seconds.

52
00:03:21,594 --> 00:03:23,007
Find its acceleration.

53
00:03:23,457 --> 00:03:24,651
Pause the video now.

54
00:03:25,101 --> 00:03:30,958
Nine minus three is six. Six divided by
four is one point five meters per second

55
00:03:30,958 --> 00:03:31,544
squared.

56
00:03:31,844 --> 00:03:34,255
A picture can say more than a page of
numbers.

57
00:03:34,705 --> 00:03:40,313
In physics, two graphs tell the story of
almost any motion: position versus time,

58
00:03:40,313 --> 00:03:42,043
and velocity versus time.

59
00:03:43,193 --> 00:03:46,265
On a position time graph, the slope is the
velocity.

60
00:03:46,715 --> 00:03:51,323
A steep line means fast. A flat line means
the object is standing still.

61
00:03:51,773 --> 00:03:54,568
And a line sloping down means it's moving
backward.

62
00:03:55,718 --> 00:03:59,174
Here, a walker moves forward six meters in
three seconds.

63
00:03:59,624 --> 00:04:04,514
The slope is six over three, which is two
meters per second. That's the walker's

64
00:04:04,514 --> 00:04:05,064
velocity.

65
00:04:06,214 --> 00:04:09,585
A curved position graph means the velocity
is changing.

66
00:04:10,035 --> 00:04:13,555
When the curve gets steeper and steeper,
the object is speeding up.

67
00:04:14,705 --> 00:04:18,118
On a velocity time graph, the slope is the
acceleration.

68
00:04:18,568 --> 00:04:23,957
A flat line means constant velocity. A
line rising upward means the object is

69
00:04:23,957 --> 00:04:24,797
speeding up.

70
00:04:25,947 --> 00:04:30,811
There's one more trick. The area under a
velocity time graph is the displacement.

71
00:04:31,261 --> 00:04:36,061
A car moving at ten meters per second for
four seconds sweeps out a rectangle.

72
00:04:36,511 --> 00:04:41,461
Its area is ten times four, which is
forty. So the car travels forty meters.

73
00:04:42,611 --> 00:04:43,976
Now try one yourself.

74
00:04:44,426 --> 00:04:49,729
A velocity time graph is a straight line
from zero up to twelve meters per second,

75
00:04:49,729 --> 00:04:54,320
over six seconds. What is the
acceleration, and how far does the object

76
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:54,773
travel?

77
00:04:55,223 --> 00:04:56,417
Pause the video now.

78
00:04:56,867 --> 00:05:02,214
The slope is twelve over six, which is two
meters per second squared. The area is a

79
00:05:02,214 --> 00:05:06,851
triangle: one half, times six, times
twelve, which is thirty six meters.

80
00:05:07,151 --> 00:05:10,308
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

81
00:05:11,458 --> 00:05:13,613
A velocity-time graph has three parts.

82
00:05:14,063 --> 00:05:19,290
First, the velocity rises from zero to ten
meters per second in four seconds.

83
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:24,497
Then it stays at ten for six seconds. Then
it drops to zero in two seconds.

84
00:05:24,947 --> 00:05:28,616
Find the acceleration in each part, and
the total displacement.

85
00:05:29,766 --> 00:05:33,201
On a velocity-time graph, the slope is the
acceleration.

86
00:05:33,651 --> 00:05:36,104
And the area under the graph is the
displacement.

87
00:05:37,254 --> 00:05:42,943
Part one: slope ten over four, two point
five meters per second squared. Area: a

88
00:05:42,943 --> 00:05:46,854
triangle, one half times four times ten,
twenty meters.

89
00:05:47,304 --> 00:05:52,897
Part two: flat, so the slope is zero.
Area: a rectangle, six times ten, sixty

90
00:05:52,897 --> 00:05:53,406
meters.

91
00:05:53,856 --> 00:05:59,527
Part three: slope minus ten over two,
minus five. Area: one half times two times

92
00:05:59,527 --> 00:06:00,661
ten, ten meters.

93
00:06:01,111 --> 00:06:04,354
Total: twenty plus sixty plus ten is
ninety meters.

94
00:06:05,504 --> 00:06:10,187
The accelerations are two point five,
zero, and minus five. The total

95
00:06:10,187 --> 00:06:12,224
displacement is ninety meters.

96
00:06:12,674 --> 00:06:18,028
Tip: a flat line on a velocity graph means
constant velocity, not standing still.

97
00:06:18,478 --> 00:06:22,894
Tip: split odd shapes into triangles and
rectangles, then add the areas.

98
00:06:24,044 --> 00:06:29,231
Your turn. On a velocity-time graph, an
object speeds up from zero to eight meters

99
00:06:29,231 --> 00:06:34,481
per second in two seconds, then stays at
eight for three more seconds. How far does

100
00:06:34,481 --> 00:06:34,860
it go?

101
00:06:35,310 --> 00:06:36,505
Pause the video now.

102
00:06:36,955 --> 00:06:42,071
The triangle is one half times two times
eight, eight meters. The rectangle is

103
00:06:42,071 --> 00:06:46,662
three times eight, twenty four meters. The
total is thirty two meters.

104
00:06:46,962 --> 00:06:51,549
When acceleration is constant, motion
follows a small set of equations.

105
00:06:51,999 --> 00:06:56,308
With them, you can predict where something
will be, and how fast it will be going.

106
00:06:57,458 --> 00:06:58,845
We use five quantities.

107
00:06:59,295 --> 00:07:04,903
The displacement, delta x. The initial
velocity, v naught. The final velocity, v.

108
00:07:04,903 --> 00:07:07,465
The acceleration, a. And the time, t.

109
00:07:08,615 --> 00:07:12,477
The first equation comes straight from the
definition of acceleration.

110
00:07:12,927 --> 00:07:14,783
v equals v naught, plus a t.

111
00:07:15,933 --> 00:07:18,343
The second equation gives the
displacement.

112
00:07:18,793 --> 00:07:22,399
Delta x equals v naught t, plus one half a
t squared.

113
00:07:22,849 --> 00:07:27,350
It's the area under the velocity time
graph: a rectangle, plus a triangle.

114
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,377
The third equation leaves out time.

115
00:07:30,827 --> 00:07:34,326
v squared equals v naught squared, plus
two a delta x.

116
00:07:35,476 --> 00:07:38,527
Notice that each equation is missing one
quantity.

117
00:07:38,977 --> 00:07:43,137
Pick the equation that leaves out the
quantity you don't know, and don't need.

118
00:07:44,287 --> 00:07:49,367
Let's try an example. A car starts from
rest, and accelerates at three meters per

119
00:07:49,367 --> 00:07:52,628
second squared for four seconds. How far
does it go?

120
00:07:53,078 --> 00:07:58,177
We know v naught, a, and t, and we want
delta x. So we use the second equation.

121
00:07:58,627 --> 00:08:03,930
Delta x equals zero, plus one half times
three times four squared, which is twenty

122
00:08:03,930 --> 00:08:04,707
four meters.

123
00:08:05,857 --> 00:08:07,222
Now try one yourself.

124
00:08:07,672 --> 00:08:12,882
A skateboard moving at two meters per
second accelerates at one meter per second

125
00:08:12,882 --> 00:08:17,571
squared, over a distance of six meters.
How fast is it going at the end?

126
00:08:18,021 --> 00:08:19,215
Pause the video now.

127
00:08:19,665 --> 00:08:24,845
Time isn't given, so use the third
equation. v squared equals two squared,

128
00:08:24,845 --> 00:08:30,375
plus two times one times six, which is
sixteen. So v is four meters per second.

129
00:08:30,674 --> 00:08:33,831
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

130
00:08:34,981 --> 00:08:40,074
A car moving at twenty meters per second
brakes, slowing at four meters per second

131
00:08:40,074 --> 00:08:40,571
squared.

132
00:08:41,021 --> 00:08:44,882
How long does it take to stop, and how far
does it travel while braking?

133
00:08:46,032 --> 00:08:49,979
List what you know. Stopping means the
final velocity is zero.

134
00:08:50,429 --> 00:08:53,543
For the time, use v equals v zero plus a
t.

135
00:08:53,993 --> 00:08:59,487
For the distance without using the time,
use v squared equals v zero squared plus

136
00:08:59,487 --> 00:09:00,436
two a delta x.

137
00:09:01,586 --> 00:09:05,831
Time: zero equals twenty minus four t, so
t is five seconds.

138
00:09:06,281 --> 00:09:11,913
Distance: zero equals four hundred minus
eight delta x, so delta x is fifty meters.

139
00:09:12,363 --> 00:09:17,354
Check with the first equation: twenty
times five, minus one half times four

140
00:09:17,354 --> 00:09:22,411
times twenty five. That's one hundred
minus fifty, fifty meters. It matches.

141
00:09:23,561 --> 00:09:26,676
The car stops in five seconds, after fifty
meters.

142
00:09:27,126 --> 00:09:32,103
Tip: braking means a is negative when the
car moves in the positive direction.

143
00:09:32,103 --> 00:09:34,401
Leaving out the sign gives nonsense.

144
00:09:34,851 --> 00:09:39,245
Tip: pick the equation that leaves out the
quantity you don't know and don't need.

145
00:09:40,395 --> 00:09:45,206
Your turn. The same car is going thirty
meters per second, with the same braking.

146
00:09:45,206 --> 00:09:46,987
What is its stopping distance?

147
00:09:47,437 --> 00:09:48,632
Pause the video now.

148
00:09:49,082 --> 00:09:54,476
Zero equals nine hundred minus eight delta
x, so delta x is one hundred twelve point

149
00:09:54,476 --> 00:09:59,485
five meters. Going one and a half times as
fast more than doubles the stopping

150
00:09:59,485 --> 00:10:01,733
distance, because speed is squared.

151
00:10:02,033 --> 00:10:06,906
Drop a ball and a feather in a chamber
with no air, and they land at exactly the

152
00:10:06,906 --> 00:10:07,516
same time.

153
00:10:07,966 --> 00:10:12,403
Without air resistance, every object falls
with the same acceleration.

154
00:10:13,553 --> 00:10:18,572
Near Earth's surface, that acceleration is
about nine point eight meters per second

155
00:10:18,572 --> 00:10:20,508
squared, pointing straight down.

156
00:10:20,958 --> 00:10:22,003
We call it g.

157
00:10:23,153 --> 00:10:27,142
Free fall means the only force acting on
an object is gravity.

158
00:10:27,592 --> 00:10:32,392
That's motion with constant acceleration,
so the kinematic equations still work.

159
00:10:32,842 --> 00:10:38,285
We use y for vertical position, and take
the acceleration to be minus g, because it

160
00:10:38,285 --> 00:10:39,072
points down.

161
00:10:40,222 --> 00:10:41,800
Let's drop a stone from rest.

162
00:10:42,250 --> 00:10:47,401
After one second, it's falling at nine
point eight meters per second. After two

163
00:10:47,401 --> 00:10:49,226
seconds, nineteen point six.

164
00:10:49,676 --> 00:10:54,050
Every second, it gains another nine point
eight meters per second of speed.

165
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:57,205
How far does it fall in three seconds?

166
00:10:57,655 --> 00:11:01,964
It starts from rest, so the distance
fallen is one half g t squared.

167
00:11:02,414 --> 00:11:07,470
One half, times nine point eight, times
three squared, is about forty four meters.

168
00:11:08,620 --> 00:11:12,204
Now throw a ball straight up. Gravity
slows it on the way up.

169
00:11:12,654 --> 00:11:16,388
At the very top, its velocity is zero, for
just an instant.

170
00:11:16,838 --> 00:11:21,638
But its acceleration is still g, pointing
down. Gravity never switches off.

171
00:11:22,788 --> 00:11:24,153
Now try one yourself.

172
00:11:24,603 --> 00:11:29,882
A ball is thrown straight up at nineteen
point six meters per second. How long does

173
00:11:29,882 --> 00:11:31,472
it take to reach the top?

174
00:11:31,922 --> 00:11:33,117
Pause the video now.

175
00:11:33,567 --> 00:11:39,607
At the top, v is zero. So zero equals
nineteen point six, minus nine point eight

176
00:11:39,607 --> 00:11:42,250
t. That gives t equals two seconds.

177
00:11:42,550 --> 00:11:45,707
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

178
00:11:46,857 --> 00:11:52,111
A ball is thrown straight up at fourteen
point seven meters per second. Use g

179
00:11:52,111 --> 00:11:53,748
equals nine point eight.

180
00:11:54,198 --> 00:11:58,875
Find the time to the top, the maximum
height, and the total time until it comes

181
00:11:58,875 --> 00:11:59,467
back down.

182
00:12:00,617 --> 00:12:05,929
Take up as positive. Then the acceleration
is minus nine point eight, the whole time.

183
00:12:06,379 --> 00:12:09,537
At the very top, the velocity is zero for
an instant.

184
00:12:09,987 --> 00:12:15,833
Use v equals v zero plus a t for the time,
and v squared equals v zero squared plus

185
00:12:15,833 --> 00:12:17,453
two a y for the height.

186
00:12:18,603 --> 00:12:24,593
Time to the top: zero equals fourteen
point seven minus nine point eight t. So t

187
00:12:24,593 --> 00:12:26,539
is one point five seconds.

188
00:12:26,989 --> 00:12:31,775
Height: fourteen point seven squared,
divided by two times nine point eight.

189
00:12:31,775 --> 00:12:33,475
That's about eleven meters.

190
00:12:33,925 --> 00:12:37,509
The way down mirrors the way up. Total
time: three seconds.

191
00:12:37,959 --> 00:12:42,762
And it lands at the same speed it was
thrown, fourteen point seven meters per

192
00:12:42,762 --> 00:12:44,508
second, but now moving down.

193
00:12:45,658 --> 00:12:51,068
The ball rises for one point five seconds,
reaches about eleven meters, and is back

194
00:12:51,068 --> 00:12:52,698
down after three seconds.

195
00:12:53,148 --> 00:12:58,596
Tip: at the top, the velocity is zero, but
the acceleration is not. It's still nine

196
00:12:58,596 --> 00:12:59,975
point eight downward.

197
00:13:00,425 --> 00:13:04,051
Tip: decide which way is positive at the
start, and stick with it.

198
00:13:05,201 --> 00:13:10,450
Your turn. A stone is dropped from a cliff
forty four point one meters high. How long

199
00:13:10,450 --> 00:13:13,969
does it take to hit the ground, and how
fast is it going?

200
00:13:14,419 --> 00:13:15,614
Pause the video now.

201
00:13:16,064 --> 00:13:21,622
Forty four point one equals four point
nine t squared, so t squared is nine, and

202
00:13:21,622 --> 00:13:27,181
t is three seconds. The speed is nine
point eight times three, twenty nine point

203
00:13:27,181 --> 00:13:28,779
four meters per second.

204
00:13:29,078 --> 00:13:33,815
Now let's put the whole unit together,
with one bigger problem that uses every

205
00:13:33,815 --> 00:13:35,030
idea from this unit.

206
00:13:36,180 --> 00:13:38,889
A model rocket launches straight up from
the ground.

207
00:13:39,339 --> 00:13:44,307
Its engine gives it a steady upward
acceleration of twenty meters per second

208
00:13:44,307 --> 00:13:47,510
squared, for two seconds. Then the fuel
runs out.

209
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:51,821
After that, it's in free fall. Use g
equals nine point eight.

210
00:13:52,271 --> 00:13:56,619
Find the speed and height when the fuel
runs out, the maximum height, and the

211
00:13:56,619 --> 00:13:57,861
total time in the air.

212
00:13:59,011 --> 00:14:04,703
There are two stages, so solve them one at
a time. Stage one: constant acceleration

213
00:14:04,703 --> 00:14:05,389
from rest.

214
00:14:05,839 --> 00:14:10,969
Stage two starts where stage one ends.
It's free fall, with a equals minus nine

215
00:14:10,969 --> 00:14:11,749
point eight.

216
00:14:12,199 --> 00:14:14,695
Then the fall from the top is a drop from
rest.

217
00:14:15,845 --> 00:14:17,338
Part a, the powered stage.

218
00:14:17,788 --> 00:14:21,969
Speed at burnout: twenty times two, forty
meters per second, upward.

219
00:14:22,419 --> 00:14:26,793
Height at burnout: one half times twenty
times two squared, forty meters.

220
00:14:27,243 --> 00:14:28,544
Part b, coasting up.

221
00:14:28,994 --> 00:14:34,061
Extra height: forty squared over two times
nine point eight, about eighty one point

222
00:14:34,061 --> 00:14:34,733
six meters.

223
00:14:35,183 --> 00:14:39,700
Maximum height: forty plus eighty one
point six, about one hundred twenty two

224
00:14:39,700 --> 00:14:40,111
meters.

225
00:14:40,561 --> 00:14:45,318
Time to coast up: forty over nine point
eight, about four point one seconds.

226
00:14:45,768 --> 00:14:47,069
Part c, falling back.

227
00:14:47,519 --> 00:14:53,095
Fall from one hundred twenty two meters: t
equals the square root of two H over g,

228
00:14:53,095 --> 00:14:55,135
about five point zero seconds.

229
00:14:55,585 --> 00:15:00,752
Total: two plus four point one plus five
point zero, about eleven point one

230
00:15:00,752 --> 00:15:01,303
seconds.

231
00:15:02,453 --> 00:15:07,518
The rocket burns out at forty meters per
second and forty meters up, peaks at about

232
00:15:07,518 --> 00:15:11,669
one hundred twenty two meters, and lands
after about eleven seconds.

233
00:15:12,119 --> 00:15:16,674
On a velocity-time graph, the slope is
plus twenty during the burn, then minus

234
00:15:16,674 --> 00:15:19,244
nine point eight for the rest of the
flight.

235
00:15:19,694 --> 00:15:24,845
Tip: the rocket keeps rising after the
engine stops. Its upward velocity takes

236
00:15:24,845 --> 00:15:25,902
time to run out.

237
00:15:26,352 --> 00:15:31,301
Tip: in multi-stage problems, the final
values of one stage are the starting

238
00:15:31,301 --> 00:15:32,539
values of the next.

239
00:15:33,689 --> 00:15:38,465
Your turn. For the whole flight, what is
the rocket's average velocity, and roughly

240
00:15:38,465 --> 00:15:39,961
what is its average speed?

241
00:15:40,411 --> 00:15:41,605
Pause the video now.

242
00:15:42,055 --> 00:15:46,295
It lands where it started, so the
displacement is zero, and the average

243
00:15:46,295 --> 00:15:50,953
velocity is zero. The distance is about
two hundred forty three meters, up and

244
00:15:50,953 --> 00:15:54,834
back, so the average speed is about twenty
two meters per second.

245
